iguez"], "rationale": "The newly drawn 6th District leans heavily Republican, the open seat removes the incumbent Democratic advantage, and Miguez's fundraising dominance plus a 93% market price point to a Republican hold. Louisiana's runoff system and a divided GOP field add some noise, but not enough to put a Democrat in contention.", "watch": "If the Republican runoff fractures badly or Miguez's residency problem escalates, a Democrat could sneak into a low-turnout runoff, though the district lean makes that a long shot.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; fundamentals point clearly one way (post-redistricting), so it is treated as safe for that party.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Cleo Fields (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's 6th District was redrawn and now leans Republican by about 16 points, and incumbent Cleo Fields is retiring to run for state office, leaving an open seat. State Senator Blake Miguez enters as the clear Republican frontrunner, having raised $6.6 million, far ahead of rivals like Rick Edmonds. The Democratic field is thin, with Lindsay Garcia raising only about $65,000.\n\nThe race has been marked by Republican infighting, including mutual residency accusations between Miguez and Edmonds and questions about Miguez's home in New Iberia. Prediction markets give Democrats only about a 6% chance. Louisiana's all-party primary and runoff system means the general contest may effectively be settled among Republicans, making this seat very likely to stay in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.024, "margin": -16.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.033, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0316, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0303, "margin": -16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0316, "margin": -15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0192, "margin": -17.